<%# decision_contract.js — THE EXPLANATION CONTRACT, and it was NOT ON THE PAGE
    until 2026-08-13. I built it, tested it, corrected the UNKNOWN-role defect in
    it, and told Cory it was "landed and unblocking B" — while the browser had
    never loaded it once. `DecisionContract` was undefined in production, so the
    renderer B is blocked on could not have been written against it.

    THE LOAD-ORDER GUARD DID NOT AND COULD NOT CATCH THIS. It derives its
    requirement from globals APP.JS CALLS, and app.js calls nothing here by
    design — the consumer is B's renderer, in another lane. So a module built FOR
    ANOTHER LANE has no caller to derive from and falls straight through the
    automatic check. Rule 14 in its purest form, on the file whose entire purpose
    is to be the thing a renderer can trust, committed by me the same week the
    rule's converse was written.

    Loaded AFTER engine.js by convention rather than by need: resolution() takes
    the engine's CFG as an ARGUMENT rather than reaching for a global, so there
    is no ordering dependency to get wrong. script_load_order.test.js now asserts
    this line explicitly, because derivation cannot. %>
<script src="/js/draft/decision_contract.js"></script>

